// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `v` in a call to `f`
// THE SIBLING OF `error-inline-shape-tyaram-elem-array.vl`, ONE CONSTRUCTOR AWAY — and it
// was check-clean INVALID WASM rather than a crash, on master as well as this branch.
//
// The `T[]` guard one clause up cannot reach this spelling. `refArrShapeKind`'s paren-union
// arm falls through UNCONDITIONALLY (`return 2` for any `( … | … )[]` whatever its arms
// name), so `nameIsRefArray("(T | null)[]")` is TRUE and the guard's shadow clause steps
// aside. `rlInternName` then resolves the unregistered `T | null` onto struct row 0, and
// the field's element type becomes the SHAPE ITSELF — a self-referential row:
//
//   (type 0  (struct (field (mut (ref 11)))))     ;; the {g: (T|null)[]} row
//   (type 10 (array  (mut (ref null 0))))         ;; its element is type 0 ITSELF
//
// with the call site lowering the literal as raw i32s into that ref array. `vl check
// --codegen` rc 0; `vl run` "expected (ref null $type), found i32".
//
// THE KIND IS WHAT SEPARATES THIS FROM THE SHADOW CASE. With a real `type T` in scope the
// ruling says the parameter binds nothing and the shape is CONCRETE, so it must still
// intern — `error-param-shadow-tyaram-nullable-elem.vl` is exactly that program and it
// must keep printing 2. `refArrShapeKind` already tells them apart: it answers 1 for a
// resolved nullable-STRUCT element and only reaches the kind-2 fall-through when nothing
// claimed it. The guard skips on kind 2 alone, so every resolved kind keeps its row —
// pinned by that fixture, by a concrete `(S | null)[]` and by a value-union
// `(i32 | string)[]`, all three of which still compile and run.
//
// Found by an adversarial review agent asked to probe spellings one constructor away from
// the pinned `T[]` fixture — the class of check the corpus cannot supply on its own.
function f<T>(v: { g: (T | null)[] }) {
  v.g.length
}

print(f({ g: [1, 2, 3] }))
